{
  "summary": "Hardens legacy-to-descriptor wallet migration against crashes, mid-process failures, and unexpected filesystem exceptions. It constructs the new SQLite database in a temporary location before replacing the original BerkeleyDB files, removes leftover journal files that could corrupt the new database, and wraps migration steps in exception handlers so failures trigger automatic rollback and restore the wallet from backup.",
  "problem": "Unexpected exceptions during migration (such as read-only directory errors or failures during auxiliary wallet creation) bypassed post-failure cleanup, leaving users with partially migrated files and requiring manual backup restoration. Additionally, MigrateToSQLite deleted original BDB databases before verifying that SQLite database creation succeeded, and preexisting journal files at the destination could be rolled back by SQLite, corrupting the migrated wallet.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'The goal of the PR is to handle failures in a controlled way. Just so the process can automatically restore the original wallet without requiring user manual intervention.'",
    "Commit e2e397edae: 'Without it, SQLite rolls the stale journal back into the migrated db once it is moved into place, writing pages of an unrelated database into it.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches wallet database manipulation and exception handling in wallet.cpp and db.cpp, accompanied by new functional tests for unwritable directories, stale journals, and cross-filesystem migration.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Refined error handling for non-writable directories and added test coverage for migrations spanning different filesystems and recovering from leftover journal files.",
  "card": "This pull request hardens legacy wallet migration to SQLite and descriptors against mid-process failures, unhandled exceptions, and stale journal files. Previously, errors or exceptions during migration bypassed cleanup logic and required manual backup restoration, while MigrateToSQLite deleted the original database before the new database was fully written and verified. This change stages the SQLite database in a temporary directory, cleans up stale journal files that could corrupt the database upon opening, and ensures any failure or exception triggers automatic rollback to the backup wallet without manual user intervention.",
